  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nHOT WITH SCATTERED SHOWERS AND STORMS THROUGH FRIDAY:\n\nHigh pressure remains centered over the Four Corners region \ntoday and tomorrow leading to unseasonably warm temperatures \nand relatively dry conditions. The exception is scattered \nafternoon showers and storms over the higher terrain with \nremnant moisture remaining in place under the high. High \ntemperatures will run 5-10 degrees above normal for late \nAugust, with some locations forecasted to warm to within a \ndegree or two of record-high temperatures. Localized flash \nflooding concerns continue near recent burn scars and\nsusceptibleterrain, although the overall risk is low. \n\nCOOLER AND WETTER THIS WEEKEND AND BEYOND: \n\nA substantial pattern change arrives Friday night as high \npressure begins to break down with a broad trough approaching \nfrom the west. Nocturnal convection is likely Friday night as \nmoisture advection intensifies with southwesterly flow aloft. \nThis will likely set most of eastern Utah and western Colorado \nup for a cloudy Saturday with chances for showers and storms as\nPWATs surge to ~175% of normal. Similar conditions remain in \nplace on Sunday as well. The uptick in mositure will increase \nthe potential for flash flooding across the region, but if \nskies remain cloudy through much of the weekend, thunderstorms \nwill be weaker than those that are able to tap into some more \nefficient daytime heating.\n\nEnsembles support PWATs of >150% of normal through Monday, \nbefore a slight downturn in moisture for Tuesday and potentially\nWednesday. Although an increase in model spread begins to lower\nforecast confidence by mid week, another solid push of \nmonsoonal moisture moves into the Southwest later in the week. \nAn eastern pacific tropical system is also possible next week, \nso we'll also be track that to see if we can tap into any of \nthat rich tropical moisture later in the week. Stay tuned!",
